Thanks for the report [USER] 

This is the same bug as #17011. The fix is already on develop in #17039 ([Preserve dialogs across parent fragment reruns](https://github.com/streamlit/streamlit/pull/17039)). We re-ran this issue’s repro against current develop after that merge: Increment updates Count as expected.

It will ship in the next release after 1.65 (next week). Until then, a workaround is to open the dialog from the parent fragment body (e.g. a session-state flag) rather than from on_click, so the dialog fragment is re-registered on the parent rerun.

For example:

```
@st.fragment
def counter_dialog():
    @st.dialog("Counter", width="small")
    def _open_dialog():
        if "count" not in st.session_state:
            st.session_state.count = 0
        st.metric("Count", st.session_state.count)
        if st.button("Increment", type="primary"):
            st.session_state.count += 1
            st.rerun(scope="fragment")
    # Open from the fragment body, not on_click.
    if st.button("Open Counter"):
        st.session_state.show_counter = True
    if st.session_state.get("show_counter"):
        _open_dialog()
counter_dialog()
```

This workaround is included in the repro app as well: https://issues.streamlit.app/?issue=gh-17112

[USER] Is there a plan to provide a finer control of the cache warnings ? Otherwise, should I just monkeypatch the `get_storage_manager` method to silence the warning ?

```python
import streamlit as st

def monkeypatch_get_storage_manager():
    if st.runtime.exists():
        return st.runtime.get_instance().cache_storage_manager
    else:
        # When running in "raw mode", we can't access the CacheStorageManager,
        # so we're falling back to InMemoryCache.
        # _LOGGER.warning("No runtime found, using MemoryCacheStorageManager")
        return st.runtime.caching.storage.dummy_cache_storage.MemoryCacheStorageManager()

st.runtime.caching._data_caches.get_storage_manager = monkeypatch_get_storage_manager
```

I think you should be able to handle this today by just not executing anything that would show up in the sidebar. E.g. like in the example below where you're only showing `st.navigation` and `st.sidebar.write` if the user is logged in. If not, you're not showing the sidebar but just a login screen:

```python
if st.user.is_logged_in:
    st.navigation(...)
    st.sidebar.write("Welcome to the app!")
else:
    if st.button("Login"):
        st.user.login()
```

I'll leave this open but it's unlikely we'll add a way to not show the sidebar. Security-wise the best approach is to not even execute any code that could show something sensitive if the user isn't logged in.

